+//! The audit: what the cluster committed is what every node holds.
+//!
+//! `meshkv/5.22-consistency-audit` is the last step of the track, and it is the one that closes the
+//! loop the whole course opened: every step has argued that *a decision survives* — a vote, a commit,
+//! a snapshot, a membership change, a partition, a restart, an upgrade. The audit is how that is
+//! checked after the fact, from the outside, without trusting any single node's story.
+//!
+//! Each node is asked two things: **how far it has applied**, and **what its state is** at that
+//! position (a digest, `5.10`'s). From the answers the audit derives three facts:
+//!
+//! * **the common prefix.** The smallest applied index across the cluster is a position every node
+//!   has reached. Everything at or below it is what the cluster decided; the audit's job is to show
+//!   that every node agrees about it.
+//! * **who is behind.** A node whose applied index is above the common prefix is not wrong — it is
+//!   ahead, which is fine; a node *below* it defines the prefix. Being behind is not a finding; being
+//!   *stuck* is, and so is being behind after a heal, which is what a runbook watches.
+//! * **who diverged.** A node that reports the same applied index as another and a different digest
+//!   has a different state at the same position. That is the failure the whole track exists to
+//!   prevent, and it is the one fact that makes the audit say *unhealthy*.
+//!
+//! The digest comparison is done at the *common prefix* only: two nodes at different indices cannot
+//! be compared, and pretending they can would make every audit of a busy cluster fail. A node ahead of
+//! the prefix has nothing to disagree about yet — it will be compared when the others catch up.
+//!
+//! The audit is a value, not a script: `audit(states)` answers a `Report`, and the report is what a
+//! runbook prints. The last property is the one that matters for a drill: the report has to be able to
+//! say *why* it is unhappy in a sentence an operator can act on, not a table nobody reads.
+
