+# The consistency audit
+
+Every step of this track has argued that a decision survives something: a vote survives a lost node, a
+commit survives a partition, a snapshot survives a restart, a membership change survives its window, an
+upgrade survives two builds. This page is the check that the argument held — from the outside, after
+the fact, without trusting any single node's story.
+
+## What a node is asked
+
+Two things:
+
+- **how far it has applied** (`5.10`'s index);
+- **what its state is** at that index — a digest, and one that is a function of the entries and
+  nothing else.
+
+## What the audit derives
+
+| fact | how |
+| --- | --- |
+| the common prefix | the smallest applied index across the answers |
+| who diverged | nodes *at* the prefix whose digest is not the majority's |
+| who is behind | nodes below the prefix — by definition none, since the prefix is the minimum; the field exists for a collector that answers a stale set |
+
+The comparison happens **at the common prefix only**. A node ahead of it cannot be compared yet — it
+has applied entries the others have not — and pretending it could would make every audit of a busy
+cluster fail. Being ahead is what a leader looks like; being *different at the same index* is the
+failure the whole track exists to prevent.
+
+## The report is a sentence
+
+```text
+3 node(s) audited, all agree at index 100
+2 node(s) audited, and n3 disagrees about the state at index 100 — the cluster committed something a
+node does not hold
+```
+
+The report names the node and the index, because that is what an operator acts on: which node, and
+where. A report that said "audit failed" would be a report nobody reads twice.
+
+## How it is used
+
+The audit closes the loop of every drill in this track:
+
+- after a partition (`5.14`), the majority and the minority converge to one state, and the audit says
+  so;
+- after a rolling restart (`5.15`), every node is back and holds what the cluster committed;
+- after an upgrade (`5.21`), two builds have one state, not two.
+
+It is also the step's own graduation exercise: a cluster the previous twenty-one steps built, audited
+by a function the last one wrote, and the answer is one sentence.
